{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "description": "Use for ARC puzzle solving.", "tags": [], "related_skills": [], "content": "---\nname: arc-grid-reasoning\ndescription: Use for ARC puzzle solving.\n---\n\n# ARC Grid Reasoning\n\nUse for ARC-like grid puzzles involving noisy backgrounds, repeated shapes, recoloring, symmetry, and demonstrations.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Record exact dimensions, colors, and changed cells from each demo. Use coordinates or a script instead of visual impressions alone.\n2. Identify background and foreground objects; group connected components and infer each object's role from demos.\n3. Extract shapes as exact occupied coordinate sets relative to bounding-box origin, preserving holes and irregular edges.\n4. Test candidate rules against demos. Require exact changed-cell agreement; consider rotations/reflections only if supported.\n5. Re-extract the exemplar from every fresh test input. A demo establishes the rule, not necessarily a reusable stencil.\n6. Apply only required changes. Verify dimensions, cell range, row lengths, and preservation of untouched cells. Revisit shape, object role, symmetry, and overlap after incorrect feedback.\n\n## Repeated-shape recoloring\nWhen a distinctive colored object acts as a stencil and matching background patterns are recolored to its color, locate exact occupied-cell matches. Keep the exemplar and unrelated cells unchanged. See `references/stencil-matching.md` for a procedure and demo-derived example.\n\n## Pitfalls\n- Do not reuse a demo stencil without checking the fresh test's exemplar.\n- Do not include neighboring noise or omit cells from the stencil.\n- Do not fill an object's bounding rectangle; match its exact mask.\n- Investigate overlapping candidate matches instead of silently dropping them.\n- Do not assume rotations or reflections are allowed without evidence.\n- Prefer mechanical validation over hand-transcribing a full grid.\n", "path": "reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/SKILL.md